  "ja-salaryman": {
    "word": "サラリーマン",
    "url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E3%82%B5%E3%83%A9%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC%E3%83%9E%E3%83%B3",
    "retrievedAt": "2026-11-10T12:29:04.717622+00:00",
    "revisionIds": ["92274434"],
    "language": "Japanese",
    "html": "<h2 id=\"Japanese\">Japanese</h2>\n\n<h3 id=\"Etymology\">Etymology</h3>\n<p>First cited to 1924 in the novel <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naomi_(novel)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Naomi (novel)\">Chijin no Ai</a></i> (published in English as <i>Naomi</i>) by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jun%27ichiro_Tanizaki\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Jun&#39;ichiro Tanizaki\">Jun’ichirō Tanizaki</a>.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-NKD&#95;1-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-NKD-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup>\n</p>\n<p>Although often described as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/wasei_eigo\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:wasei eigo\">wasei eigo</a> (<i class=\"Jpan mention\" lang=\"ja\"><a href=\"/wiki/%E5%92%8C%E8%A3%BD%E8%8B%B1%E8%AA%9E#Japanese\" title=\"和製英語\">和製英語</a></i>), this appears to derive from the <span class=\"etyl\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:English language\">English</a></span> phrase <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"en\"><a href=\"/wiki/salaried#English\" title=\"salaried\">salaried</a> <a href=\"/wiki/man#English\" title=\"man\">man</a></i>,<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-NKD&#95;1-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-NKD-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-2\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-DJR&#95;3-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-DJR-3\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>3<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> which appears in print at least as early as 1828. Additionally, the English noun phrase “<a href=\"/w/index.php?title=salary_man&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"salary man (page does not exist)\">salary man</a>” also predates the Japanese term. The resulting Japanese term was reimported into English as a compound in the late 20th century.\n</p>\n<p>The form is a regular phonological clipping of the borrowed phrase, dropping the English past‐participle suffix to fit Japanese phonotactics, analyzable as <i class=\"Jpan mention\" lang=\"ja\"><a href=\"/wiki/%E3%82%B5%E3%83%A9%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC#Japanese\" title=\"サラリー\">サラリー</a></i> <span class=\"mention-gloss-paren annotation-paren\">(</span><span class=\"mention-tr tr\">sararī</span><span class=\"mention-gloss-paren annotation-paren\">)</span> +&#8206; <i class=\"Jpan mention\" lang=\"ja\"><a href=\"/wiki/%E3%83%9E%E3%83%B3#Japanese\" title=\"マン\">マン</a></i> <span class=\"mention-gloss-paren annotation-paren\">(</span><span class=\"mention-tr tr\">-man</span><span class=\"mention-gloss-paren annotation-paren\">)</span>.\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"Pronunciation\">Pronunciation</h3>\n",
